REV.page({"course":"python","id":"w08","title":"File handling","summary":"Week 8 as on Seek, one section per lecture. Why files: RAM against the hard disk (8.1); writing a file and reading it back (8.2); searching a big file line by line (8.3); very big files, a line or a character at a time (8.4); a Caesar cipher on a whole book (8.5); and read(n), seek and a genome search (8.6).","n":8,"lectures":[{"code":"8.1","title":"Introduction to File Handling","video":"o5MBF0yo6lA","seconds":705},{"code":"8.2","title":"Reading and Writing to a File","video":"rYLJaAdgLhI","seconds":837},{"code":"8.3","title":"Big text file handling","video":"9eOxi7smTKg","seconds":798},{"code":"8.4","title":"Very big files - a tip","video":"NwFpo_KGvOA","seconds":319},{"code":"8.5","title":"Caesar Cipher","video":"MkqFvnC2naE","seconds":991},{"code":"8.6","title":"File handling, Genetic Sequences","video":"df8FxNd27Dg","seconds":746},{"code":"8.7","title":"Extra: Introduction | advanced concepts & exception handling","video":"4HgUCharOBw","seconds":147,"extra":true}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"lecture-8-1-introduction-to-file-handling","level":2,"title":"Lecture 8.1: Introduction to File Handling"},{"id":"lecture-8-2-reading-and-writing-to-a-file","level":2,"title":"Lecture 8.2: Reading and Writing to a File"},{"id":"lecture-8-3-big-text-file-handling","level":2,"title":"Lecture 8.3: Big text file handling"},{"id":"lecture-8-4-very-big-files-a-tip","level":2,"title":"Lecture 8.4: Very big files - a tip"},{"id":"lecture-8-5-caesar-cipher","level":2,"title":"Lecture 8.5: Caesar Cipher"},{"id":"lecture-8-6-file-handling-genetic-sequences","level":2,"title":"Lecture 8.6: File handling, Genetic Sequences"},{"id":"jumping-inside-a-file-read-n-and-seek","level":3,"title":"Jumping inside a file: read(n) and seek"},{"id":"searching-a-genome-with-in","level":3,"title":"Searching a genome with in"},{"id":"extra-lecture-not-on-seek-this-term","level":2,"title":"Extra lecture (not on Seek this term)"},{"id":"the-week-exceptions-and-functional-programming","level":3,"title":"The week: exceptions and functional programming"},{"id":"error-messages-of-this-week","level":2,"title":"Error messages of this week"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Why files</strong> (8.1): the RAM is fast and small, and it forgets everything when the power goes off; the hard disk is slow and big, and it keeps its data. When the data is too big to type, a program reads it from a file on the disk.</li>\n<li><strong>Write and read</strong> (8.2): <code>f=<wbr>open(<wbr>'mytext.<wbr>txt',<wbr>'w')</code> creates a file; <code>f.<wbr>write(<wbr>'IIT ')</code> adds text and gives back the number of characters (here 4); <code>f.close()</code> finishes. <code>x=<wbr>open(<wbr>'mytext.<wbr>txt',<wbr>'r')</code> and <code>s=x.read()</code> give the whole file as one string. A new line needs the character <code>'\\n'</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Big files</strong> (8.4, 8.3, 8.6): a 12 GB file cannot be opened in an editor, but <code>f.readline()</code> reads it one line at a time, <code>f.read(1)</code> one character at a time, and <code>f.seek(4)</code> jumps to position 4. At the end of a file, <code>readline()</code> and <code>read(1)</code> give the empty string <code>''</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Two programs on files</strong> (8.5, 8.3): a <strong>Caesar cipher</strong> builds a dictionary with <code>d[<wbr>l[i]]=<wbr>l[<wbr>(i+3)%26]</code> and writes <code>d[c]</code> for every character <code>c</code> of a book; a <strong>phone search</strong> reads line after line until <code>''</code>, and a <code>flag</code> remembers whether the number was found.</li>\n<li><strong>Errors</strong> (the extra lecture, and the whole week): exception handling is “industry standard”; it is taught in lecture 9.11. This week shows four error messages, from <code>ValueError: I/<wbr>O operation on closed file.</code> to <code>ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lecture-8-1-introduction-to-file-handling\">Lecture 8.1: Introduction to File Handling</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span><span class=\"box-t\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The RAM is fast and small, and it forgets everything when the power goes off; the hard disk is slow and big, and it keeps its data. Data too big to type is kept in a <strong>file</strong> on the disk, and a program reads it from there.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Handwritten notes.</p>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A kitchen has 100 vessels. The 30 used most often are kept on the kitchen shelf; the 70 less used ones are in a storage space outside <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>. Why not keep all 100 in the kitchen? There is not enough space, and the rarely used vessels would take “a bigger chunk of a kitchen” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>. In the same way, you keep 10 books on your own shelf and borrow from a library of 10,000 <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>. The shelf is small but easy to reach; the library is huge but not easy to reach. “As and always life is full of trade-offs” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A computer has the same two kinds of storage <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>RAM</th>\n<th>Hard disk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Also called</td>\n<td>internal memory</td>\n<td>external memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speed</td>\n<td>fast</td>\n<td>slow, “a lot slower than your RAM”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical size</td>\n<td>16 GB</td>\n<td>1 TB, about 1000 GB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>When the power goes off</td>\n<td>everything is lost</td>\n<td>the data stays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Holds</td>\n<td>what is in use right now</td>\n<td>files: music, movies, data</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The names are “from 60-70s”: internal meant on the motherboard, external meant the hard disk <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>.</li>\n<li>1 TB is “1000 GB, 1024 GB to be precise” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>.</li>\n<li>“You cannot have a lot and keep it fast” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>: like a 60-seat bus against a small two-seater that uses less fuel.</li>\n<li>Songs (MP3 files) and movies (MP4 or AVI files) live on the hard disk, which is slow but has a high capacity <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>. Even a small movie should not live in the RAM: “the moment you switch off the computer, whatever is in RAM goes away”, while the hard disk keeps its data “almost forever” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why this matters in Python</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>. A search program like those of <a href=\"#/python/w04/naive-search-in-a-list\">Week 4</a> gets its numbers typed by hand:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Input the numbers:\n1, 7, 2, 9, 15, 16, 8, 1000\nEnter the number to search\n15\nElement found.\n</code></pre>\n<p>“Where is this element coming from? You are manually typing it” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>. With 1,000,000 numbers stored on the hard disk, typing is impossible <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>. The program must read them from the file, which is what the rest of the week teaches: “How to handle files using Python” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o5MBF0yo6lA&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lecture-8-2-reading-and-writing-to-a-file\">Lecture 8.2: Reading and Writing to a File</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span><span class=\"box-t\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>f = open(<wbr>'mytext.<wbr>txt', 'w')</code> creates a file, <code>f.<wbr>write(<wbr>'IIT ')</code> adds text and gives back the number of characters, and <code>f.close()</code> finishes. <code>open(<wbr>name, 'r')</code> with <code>read()</code> gives the whole file back as one string. A new line needs the character <code>'\\n'</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecturer types into <strong>IPython</strong>, an interactive Python shell: after <code>In [n]:</code> comes what he types, and after <code>Out[n]:</code> the value that Python gives back.</p>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A variable lives in the computer’s fast memory, the RAM, and it is lost when the program ends. A <strong>file</strong> is a named piece of data on the hard disk. It stays there after the program ends, even after the computer is switched off (lecture 8.1 explains why: <a href=\"#/python/w08/lecture-8-1-introduction-to-file-handling\">Why files?</a>). <strong>Writing</strong> to a file puts text into it. <strong>Reading</strong> from a file brings its text back into a variable. “It may appear like a completely useless exercise”, the lecturer says, “but … with time, you will realize how useful this file handling will be” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Where will the file go? Files are kept in <strong>folders</strong>, also called <strong>directories</strong>. In IPython, a line that starts with <code>!</code> is sent to the operating system as a terminal command. <code>!pwd</code> (“print working directory”) shows the folder that the shell is working in <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [1]: !pwd\n/Users/srsiyengar/pod\n</code></pre>\n<p>The new file will be created in this folder, <code>pod</code>, which is empty at the start. On Windows, the command <code>cd</code> shows the current folder <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">open, write, read and close</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><code>f=<wbr>open(<wbr>'mytext.<wbr>txt',<wbr>'w')</code> opens the file named <code>mytext.txt</code> in <strong>write mode</strong> <code>'w'</code> and gives back a <strong>file object</strong>, stored here in <code>f</code>. The program talks to the file through <code>f</code>. In write mode the file is created new and empty.</li>\n<li><code>f.<wbr>write(<wbr>text)</code> adds the string <code>text</code> to the file and gives back the number of characters it wrote.</li>\n<li><code>x=<wbr>open(<wbr>'mytext.<wbr>txt',<wbr>'r')</code> opens a file that already exists, in <strong>read mode</strong> <code>'r'</code>.</li>\n<li><code>x.read()</code> gives the whole content of the file as one string (<code>str</code>).</li>\n<li><code>f.close()</code> closes the file. After that, the file object can no longer be used.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s first session <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [2]: f=open('mytext.txt','w')\n\nIn [3]: f.write('Sudarshan ')\nOut[3]: 10\n\nIn [4]: f.write('yourname ')\nOut[4]: 9\n\nIn [5]: f.write('IIT ')\nOut[5]: 4\n\nIn [6]: f.write('python ')\nOut[6]: 7\n\nIn [7]: f.write('India ')\nOut[7]: 6\n\nIn [8]: f.close()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Line by line:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>In [2]</code> creates <code>mytext.txt</code> in the folder <code>pod</code> and calls it <code>f</code>: “open a text file by name this and in the writing mode … assign that to f, as simple as that” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>.</li>\n<li><code>In [3]</code> to <code>In [7]</code> each add one word and a space. The numbers are “simply the bytes that you are writing” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>. A <strong>byte</strong> is the basic unit of storage; here every letter and every space takes one byte, so the number is also the number of characters.</li>\n<li><code>In [8]</code> closes the file: “the file gets closed and it gets written as well” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>. Python may keep written text in memory until the file is closed, so always close it.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Text written</th>\n<th>Letters + space</th>\n<th>Out</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>'Sudarshan '</code></td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">9</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>'yourname '</code></td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n<td>9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>'IIT '</code></td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>'python '</code></td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n<td>7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>'India '</code></td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In total <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">9</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">36</span></span></span></span> characters, and the file browser shows <code>mytext.txt</code> with “36 bytes” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>. Opened in an editor, the file holds one line, <code>Sudarshan yourname IIT python India</code>, with spaces in between: “If you were to not put space, it will come continuously” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading the file back</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [10]: x=open('mytext.txt','r')\n\nIn [11]: s=x.read()\n\nIn [12]: print(s)\nSudarshan yourname IIT python India\n\nIn [13]: type(s)\nOut[13]: str\n\nIn [14]: x.close()\n\nIn [15]: s=x.read()\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nValueError                                Traceback (most recent call last)\n&lt;ipython-input-15-a16f4068bc72&gt; in &lt;module&gt;\n----&gt; 1 s=x.read()\n\nValueError: I/O operation on closed file.\n</code></pre>\n<ol>\n<li><code>In [10]</code> opens the same file, this time in read mode, as <code>x</code>. Any variable name works for a file object.</li>\n<li><code>In [11]</code>: <code>x.read()</code> takes everything in the file and gives it back as one string, stored in <code>s</code>: “whatever is in the file comes and gets attached to this string” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>.</li>\n<li><code>In [13]</code>: the type of <code>s</code> is <code>str</code>, an ordinary string, so all the string tools of Weeks 1 and 2 work on it.</li>\n<li><code>In [14]</code> closes the file. After that, “you cannot do anything with x” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>, and <code>In [15]</code> stops with a <code>ValueError</code>. <strong>I/O</strong> means input/output: here, reading from the file.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The file is read as it is now.</strong> The lecturer then changes <code>mytext.txt</code> by hand in an editor, replacing python and India with C++ and Germany, and reads it again with the same three lines <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [17]: x=open('mytext.txt','r')\n\nIn [18]: s=x.read()\n\nIn [19]: print(s)\nSudarshan yourname IIT C++ Germany\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then he types eight names into the file, one per line, and reads it once more <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [20]: x=open('mytext.txt','r')\n\nIn [21]: s=x.read()\n\nIn [22]: print(s)\nSudarshan\nAjit\nAmit\nTatsavit\nLakshmi\nBhawana\nRamya\nUday\n</code></pre>\n<p>The names come out on separate lines because the file holds, after each name, an invisible character that means “new line”. <code>read()</code> keeps it in the string, and <code>print</code> shows it as a line break.</p>\n<p><strong>Writing on two lines</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>. “Just because you stated it first line, second line, it does not come to the first line or second line” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [23]: f=open('newfile.txt','w')\n\nIn [24]: f.write('This is the first line')\nOut[24]: 22\n\nIn [25]: f.write('This is the second line')\nOut[25]: 23\n\nIn [26]: f.close()\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>newfile.txt</code> now holds one line, <code>This is the first lineThis is the second line</code>: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">22</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">23</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">45</span></span></span></span></span> characters, glued together. The fix is the <strong>newline character</strong> <code>'\\n'</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [27]: f=open('newfile.txt','w')\n\nIn [28]: f.write('This is the first line')\nOut[28]: 22\n\nIn [29]: f.write('\\n')\nOut[29]: 1\n\nIn [30]: f.write('This is the second line')\nOut[30]: 23\n\nIn [31]: f.close()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now the file shows two lines <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>This is the first line\nThis is the second line\n</code></pre>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The newline character ‘\\n’</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>'\\n'</code> (backslash n) is <strong>one</strong> character, which is why <code>f.<wbr>write(<wbr>'\\n')</code> gives back 1. The backslash and the n do not appear in the file: “This is an instruction to the file that please go to the new line” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>. It is one of the escape characters of <a href=\"#/python/w02\">Week 2</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">write adds nothing by itself</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>write</code> puts exactly the characters you give it into the file: no space between two calls, and no new line at the end. Add <code>' '</code> or <code>'\\n'</code> yourself, as the lecture does.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>The modes.</strong> “The second parameter here tells you whether you are opening the file in the writing mode or the reading mode.” “Reading means there is an existing file already … w means you are writing to the file by creating a new file” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mode</th>\n<th>If the file does not exist</th>\n<th>If the file exists</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>'r'</code>, read</td>\n<td>an error</td>\n<td>reading starts at the beginning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>'w'</code>, write</td>\n<td>a new empty file is created</td>\n<td>its old text is erased first</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Before <code>In [27]</code> the lecturer deleted <code>newfile.txt</code> in the file browser. That was not needed: opening with <code>'w'</code> empties the file anyway. Opening a missing file in read mode stops with <code>FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'nosuchfile.<wbr>txt'</code> (my own test).</p>\n<p>On the screen, the lecturer writes “r : reading, w : writing, a : ??” and leaves the third mode as homework <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYLJaAdgLhI&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>. Try it in Python before you open the answer.</p>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The lecture’s homework: what does the mode ‘a’ do?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><code>'a'</code> is <strong>append</strong> mode: the file is opened and new text is added <strong>at the end</strong>, after the old text. Nothing is erased. If the file does not exist, it is created, as with <code>'w'</code>. A small test of my own:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [1]: f=open('a.txt','w')\n\nIn [2]: f.write('IIT ')\nOut[2]: 4\n\nIn [3]: f.close()\n\nIn [4]: f=open('a.txt','a')\n\nIn [5]: f.write('Madras')\nOut[5]: 6\n\nIn [6]: f.close()\n\nIn [7]: open('a.txt','r').read()\nOut[7]: 'IIT Madras'\n</code></pre>\n<p>With <code>'w'</code> instead of <code>'a'</code> in <code>In [4]</code>, the file would hold only <code>Madras</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"lecture-8-3-big-text-file-handling\">Lecture 8.3: Big text file handling</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span><span class=\"box-t\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Searching a big file of phone numbers, one line at a time with <code>readline()</code>. At the end of the file <code>readline()</code> gives the empty string <code>''</code>, which ends the loop, and a <code>flag</code> remembers whether the number was found.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The file <code>directory.<wbr>txt</code> is 231 MB, with one 10-digit phone number per line <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>. Even with “good RAM and processor”, the editor is slow to open it and slow to close it <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>. The aim: “find a particular number in this big file” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [9]: f=open('directory.txt','r')\n\nIn [10]: f.readline()\nOut[10]: '5612152693\\n'\n\nIn [11]: f.readline()\nOut[11]: '7123173310\\n'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Each <code>readline</code> gives the next number <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>. The lecturer wants numbers, not strings <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [18]: f=open('directory.txt','r')\n\nIn [19]: s=f.readline()\n\nIn [20]: type(s)\nOut[20]: str\n\nIn [21]: n=int(s)\n\nIn [22]: type(n)\nOut[22]: int\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>int(s)</code> accepts the <code>'\\n'</code> at the end of the line: it ignores spaces and newlines around the digits.</p>\n<p><strong>What happens at the end of a file?</strong> The lecturer shows it on a tiny file <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [24]: g=open('newfile.txt','w')\n\nIn [25]: g.write('10\\n')\nOut[25]: 3\n\nIn [26]: g.write('11\\n')\nOut[26]: 3\n\nIn [27]: g.write('20\\n')\nOut[27]: 3\n\nIn [28]: g.close()\n\nIn [30]: !cat newfile.txt\n10\n11\n20\n\nIn [31]: g=open('newfile.txt','r')\n\nIn [32]: s=g.readline()\n\nIn [33]: print(s)\n10\n\nIn [34]: s=g.readline()\n\nIn [35]: print(s)\n11\n\nIn [36]: s=g.readline()\n\nIn [37]: print(s)\n20\n\nIn [38]: s=g.readline()\n\nIn [39]: print(s)\n\nIn [40]: s==''\nOut[40]: True\n</code></pre>\n<ol>\n<li><code>'10\\n'</code> is 3 characters: <code>1</code>, <code>0</code> and the newline, so <code>write</code> gives back 3.</li>\n<li><code>!cat newfile.<wbr>txt</code> shows the file with a terminal command of Linux and macOS; on Windows it is <code>type</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>.</li>\n<li>Three <code>readline</code> calls give the three lines. The fourth gives a string with nothing in it, so <code>print(s)</code> shows nothing, and <code>s==''</code> is <code>True</code>. The lecturer calls it “null”: “The moment your g read line gives you this, it means that everything is over” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">End of file</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When a file has been read to the end, <code>f.readline()</code> and <code>f.read(1)</code> give the <strong>empty string</strong> <code>''</code>, a string with no characters. A line inside the file is never <code>''</code>: even an empty line is <code>'\\n'</code>. So <code>s!=''</code> means “not at the end yet”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>The program</strong> <code>findphone.py</code>, after its first fix <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">f=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">open</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;directory.txt&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;r&#x27;</span>)\n\nflag=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>\n\ns=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;0&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">while</span>(s!=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;&#x27;</span>):\n    s=f.readline()\n    n=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(s)\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (n==<span class=\"hljs-number\">9020214197</span>):\n        <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;The number was found&quot;</span>)\n        flag=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">break</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (flag==<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;The numbmer was NOTTTTT found&quot;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The first run had no line <code>s='0'</code> and stopped at once <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>; the second run found the number:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [41]: run findphone.py\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNameError                                 Traceback (most recent call last)\n~/pod/findphone.py in &lt;module&gt;\n      3 flag=0\n      4\n----&gt; 5 while(s!=''):\n      6     s=f.readline()\n      7     n=int(s)\n\nNameError: name 's' is not defined\n\nIn [42]: run findphone.py\nThe number was found\n</code></pre>\n<p>“I should initialize s to something, otherwise it does not even enter the while loop” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>: the test <code>s!=''</code> needs a value of <code>s</code> before the first line is read, and any non-empty string will do. Then the lecturer changes the last digit to 8, <code>9020214198</code>, a number that is not in the file <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [43]: run findphone.py\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nValueError                                Traceback (most recent call last)\n~/pod/findphone.py in &lt;module&gt;\n      7 while(s!=''):\n      8     s=f.readline()\n----&gt; 9     n=int(s)\n     10     if (n==9020214198):\n     11         print(&quot;The number was found&quot;)\n\nValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''\n</code></pre>\n<p>The number is not there, so the loop reads every line. The last <code>readline</code> gives <code>''</code>, and <code>int('')</code> fails before the <code>while</code> test can stop the loop: “Whenever you try to convert something to integer, when that is null, this error comes” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>. The fix: convert only “if s is not equal to null” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>. The final program <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">f=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">open</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;directory.txt&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;r&#x27;</span>)\n\nflag=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>\n\ns=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;0&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">while</span>(s!=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;&#x27;</span>):\n    s=f.readline()\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (s!=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;&#x27;</span>):\n        n=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(s)\n        <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (n==<span class=\"hljs-number\">9020214198</span>):\n            <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;The number was found&quot;</span>)\n            flag=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\n            <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">break</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (flag==<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;The number was NOTTTTT found&quot;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [45]: run findphone.py\nThe number was NOTTTTT found\n</code></pre>\n<p>(<code>In [44]</code> still printed the misspelt “numbmer” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>.)</p>\n<p>Line by line:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>flag=0</code>: a <strong>flag</strong> is a variable that remembers whether something happened. 0 means “not found yet” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>.</li>\n<li><code>s='0'</code>: a non-empty start value, so that the first test <code>s!=''</code> is true.</li>\n<li><code>while(<wbr>s!=<wbr>''):</code> repeats until the end of the file.</li>\n<li><code>s=<wbr>f.<wbr>readline()</code> reads the next line; <code>if (s!=''):</code> skips the empty string at the end.</li>\n<li><code>n=int(s)</code> turns the line into a number. If it is the one searched for, the program prints, sets <code>flag=1</code> and leaves the loop with <code>break</code> (<a href=\"#/python/w04\">Week 4</a>).</li>\n<li>After the loop, <code>flag</code> is still 0 only if the number never matched, so “not found” is printed only then. Without the flag, “the number was found” would be followed by “the number was not found” as well <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>A trace on a small file</strong> (my own): run the final program on <code>newfile.txt</code> of this lecture (10, 11, 20), with 11 in place of the phone number.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pass</th>\n<th>Line read</th>\n<th>Equal to 11?</th>\n<th>Flag</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>start</td>\n<td>(<code>s='0'</code>)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code>'10\\n'</code>, so <code>n</code> is 10</td>\n<td>no</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code>'11\\n'</code>, so <code>n</code> is 11</td>\n<td>yes: print, <code>break</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Both lines read are not <code>''</code>, so both are converted with <code>int</code>. The program prints <code>The number was found</code>, and after the loop <code>flag==0</code> is false, so nothing more is printed. Searching 15 instead: passes 1 to 3 give 10, 11 and 20, none equal to 15; pass 4 gives <code>''</code>, so the inner <code>if</code> is skipped; the <code>while</code> test <code>'' != ''</code> is false, the loop ends with <code>flag</code> still 0, and the program prints <code>The number was NOTTTTT found</code>.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Comparing strings works too</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“This int conversion is actually not required. You can simply compare two strings” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eOxi7smTKg&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>. Then remember the newline: the line read is <code>'9020214197\\n'</code>, which is <strong>not</strong> equal to <code>'9020214197'</code>. Compare with the <code>'\\n'</code> included, or remove it first with <code>s.strip()</code> (<a href=\"#/python/w02\">Week 2</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"lecture-8-4-very-big-files-a-tip\">Lecture 8.4: Very big files - a tip</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span><span class=\"box-t\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A 12 GB file cannot be opened in an editor, but a program can read it piece by piece: <code>f.readline()</code> one line at a time, <code>f.read(1)</code> one character at a time. Then the size of the file does not matter, only the time.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A terminal on the left and IPython on the right. The folder listing (<code>ls <span>-l</span></code>) at the start <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NwFpo_KGvOA\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>-rw-r--r--@ 1 srsiyengar  staff  12258709617 Jun 27 13:35 phone_large.txt\n</code></pre>\n<p>The size is in bytes. The lecturer reads it off digit group by digit group: “8 million, 58 million, 258 million, 2 billion, 12 billion. This is 12 GB of data” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NwFpo_KGvOA\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>. The file is “a huge list of phone numbers”, one per line. Double-clicking it, the editor “is stuck … In no editor can you open this” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NwFpo_KGvOA&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>, and printing it with a terminal command “never gets over”, so he stops it with Ctrl+C <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NwFpo_KGvOA&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">How many numbers? (my arithmetic)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Each line is a 10-digit number plus the newline, <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">11</span></span></span></span></span> characters. If every line is like the first ones, the file holds <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">12258709617</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">11</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1114428147</span></span></span></span> numbers: about 1.1 billion.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Python can still read it <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NwFpo_KGvOA&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>: “No matter how big the file is, you can always read from it.”</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [4]: f=open('phone_large.txt','r')\n\nIn [5]: f.readline()\nOut[5]: '5612152693\\n'\n\nIn [6]: f.readline()\nOut[6]: '7123173310\\n'\n\nIn [7]: f.readline()\nOut[7]: '8197626055\\n'\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecturer calls <code>f.readline()</code> up to <code>In [17]</code>, each time getting the next number. Then he reads 10,000 lines in one go <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NwFpo_KGvOA&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [18]: for i in range(10000):\n    ...:     s=f.readline()\n    ...:     print(s)\n    ...:\n</code></pre>\n<ol>\n<li><code>open</code> does not load the file: it only prepares to read it from the start.</li>\n<li>Each <code>f.readline()</code> gives the next line as a string that <strong>ends with <code>'\\n'</code></strong>. The file object remembers how far it has read, just like <code>next</code> and the basket of 7.3.</li>\n<li>The loop runs 10,000 times. Each time, <code>s</code> is the next line, and <code>print(s)</code> shows it. Because <code>s</code> already ends with <code>'\\n'</code> and <code>print</code> adds its own line break, an empty line appears after every number on the screen (<code>print(<wbr>s, end=<wbr>'')</code>, from <a href=\"#/python/w03\">Week 3</a>, would avoid it).</li>\n<li>“You can keep doing it, the next 10,000, the next 10,000” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NwFpo_KGvOA&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>; the lecturer runs the loop again as <code>In [19]</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">readline</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>f.readline()</code> reads one line of the file, from where the last read stopped up to and including the next <code>'\\n'</code>, and gives it as a string. Only that one line is brought into memory, so the size of the file does not matter.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Why it never hangs: “your file system is like a big tape” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NwFpo_KGvOA&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>. Python reads it from the start, one piece after another. A 12 GB file takes a long time, “but it will never get hung”. A movie player works the same way: a movie is “a list of pictures”, “taken from your hard disk and processed one file at a time” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NwFpo_KGvOA&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>. “File handling is possible irrespective of how big the file is just that it will take a long time to process it” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NwFpo_KGvOA&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"lecture-8-5-caesar-cipher\">Lecture 8.5: Caesar Cipher</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span><span class=\"box-t\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Week 2’s cipher, now on a whole book. A dictionary maps each letter to the letter 3 places later, <code>d[<wbr>l[i]]=<wbr>l[<wbr>(i+3)%26]</code>, and the program writes <code>d[c]</code> for every character <code>c</code> it reads.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The editor vim on the left, IPython on the right.</p>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>cipher</strong> is a way to write a message in secret. In the <strong>Caesar cipher</strong>, each letter is replaced by the letter 3 places later in the alphabet: a becomes d, b becomes e, c becomes f <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>. At the end of the alphabet it wraps around to the start: w becomes z, x becomes a, y becomes b, z becomes c <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>. <a href=\"#/python/w02/lecture-2-7-an-interesting-cipher-more-on-strings\">Week 2</a> (lecture 2.7) did this on strings, with any shift <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span></span></span></span>;</span> “right now we will do it on a file” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The input is <code>sherlock.txt</code>, the start of <em>The Hound of the Baskervilles</em> by Arthur Conan Doyle <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>. The lecturer has removed spaces, punctuation and capitals: the file holds only lowercase letters and begins <code>chaptermrsherlockholmesmrsherlockholmeswhowasusually…</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>. Its size is 245519 bytes, “246 kb”.</p>\n<p><strong>The alphabet as a list.</strong> “It is always a good idea to type it on the right side and see on the interactive Python, what is happening and then code on the left side” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [2]: import string\n\nIn [3]: string.ascii_lowercase\nOut[3]: 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz'\n\nIn [4]: l=string.ascii_lowercase\n\nIn [6]: l=list(l)\n\nIn [8]: l[20]\nOut[8]: 'u'\n\nIn [9]: l[26]\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIndexError                                Traceback (most recent call last)\n&lt;ipython-input-9-49a5dcb3edba&gt; in &lt;module&gt;\n----&gt; 1 l[26]\n\nIndexError: list index out of range\n\nIn [10]: l[25]\nOut[10]: 'z'\n\nIn [11]: l[0]\nOut[11]: 'a'\n\nIn [12]: l[(24+3)%26]\nOut[12]: 'b'\n\nIn [13]: l[24]\nOut[13]: 'y'\n</code></pre>\n<ol>\n<li><code>string</code> is a library of ready-made strings; <code>string.<wbr>ascii_lowercase</code> is the 26 lowercase letters in order <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>.</li>\n<li><code>list(l)</code> splits the string into a list of 26 one-letter strings (<code>In [5]</code> and <code>In [7]</code> display <code>l</code> before and after).</li>\n<li>The indexes run from 0 (<code>'a'</code>) to 25 (<code>'z'</code>). <code>l[26]</code> does not exist: “it ends at 25” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>.</li>\n<li><code>y</code> is <code>l[24]</code>. Three places later is index <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">24</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">27</span></span></span></span>,</span> past the end. “When you do mod 26, see what you will get? You will get b” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">27</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">mod</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span>,</span> and <code>l[1]</code> is <code>'b'</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box fm\" id=\"f1\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Formula</span><span class=\"box-t\">Caesar cipher: index of the code letter</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><div class=\"md\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.854em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.05724em;\">j</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">mod</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span></span></span></span></span></div>\nwhere <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6595em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span></span></span></span></span> is the index of a letter in the alphabet list (a is 0, b is 1, …, z is 25), <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.854em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.05724em;\">j</span></span></span></span></span> is the index of its code letter, and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">mod</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span></span></span></span></span> is the remainder when <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span></span></span></span> is divided by 26, written <code>x % 26</code> in Python. For example, y has <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6595em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">24</span></span></span></span>,</span> so <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.854em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.05724em;\">j</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">27</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">mod</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span>,</span> which is b.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The remainder makes the wrap-around work: for <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7955em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">22</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7429em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7804em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25</span></span></span></span></span> and the remainder is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7429em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span></span></span></span></span> itself; for x, y, z it is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">27</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">28</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>.</span></p>\n<p><strong>The program</strong> <code>caesar.py</code>, as it is run <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;&#x27;&#x27;This program considers an input file and encrypts it by using caesar\ncipher. By that we mean, we shift the letters by 3 units. For example,\na becomes d, b becomes e and so on... w becomes z, x becomes a,\ny becomes b and z becomes c&#x27;&#x27;&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> string\n\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">create_caesar_dictionary</span>():\n    l=string.ascii_lowercase\n    l=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">list</span>(l)\n    d={}\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> i <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(l)):\n        d[l[i]]=l[(i+<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)%<span class=\"hljs-number\">26</span>]\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> d\n\n\nf=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">open</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;sherlock.txt&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;r&#x27;</span>)\ng=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">open</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;encrypted_sherlock.txt&#x27;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;w&#x27;</span>)\nd=create_caesar_dictionary()\n\nc=f.read(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">while</span> (c!=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;&#x27;</span>):\n    g.write(d[c])\n    c=f.read(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)\n\nf.close()\ng.close()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Line by line:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Lines 1–4 are a string in triple quotes that explains the program. It does nothing when the program runs; it is there for the reader <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>.</li>\n<li><code>create_<wbr>caesar_<wbr>dictionary()</code> builds the list of 26 letters, starts an empty dictionary <code>d</code>, and for each index <code>i</code> stores the code letter of <code>l[i]</code>: <code>d[<wbr>l[i]]=<wbr>l[<wbr>(i+3)%26]</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>. Then it returns <code>d</code>. (The lecturer first typed the name as <code>creat_<wbr>caesar_<wbr>dictionary</code> and corrected it: “I got the spelling of create wrong” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>.)</li>\n<li><code>f</code> is the book, opened for reading; <code>g</code> is the new file <code>encrypted_sherlock.<wbr>txt</code>, opened for writing <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>.</li>\n<li><code>c=f.read(1)</code> reads <strong>one</strong> character.</li>\n<li><code>while (c!=<wbr>''):</code> repeats as long as a character was read. At the end of the file, <code>f.read(1)</code> gives the empty string, which the lecturer calls “null space which is colon, colon, nothing in between” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>.</li>\n<li>Inside the loop, <code>g.<wbr>write(<wbr>d[c])</code> writes the code letter of <code>c</code>, and <code>c=f.read(1)</code> reads the next character <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>.</li>\n<li>Both files are closed at the end <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The dictionary loop, step by step:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>i</code></th>\n<th><code>l[i]</code></th>\n<th><code>i+3</code></th>\n<th><code>(i+3)%26</code></th>\n<th><code>d[l[i]]</code></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>a</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>d</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>b</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>e</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>c</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>f</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>…</td>\n<td>…</td>\n<td>…</td>\n<td>…</td>\n<td>…</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>22</td>\n<td>w</td>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>23</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>26</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>a</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>24</td>\n<td>y</td>\n<td>27</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>b</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>z</td>\n<td>28</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>c</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecturer checks the same loop in IPython <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [16]: d={}\n\nIn [17]: for i in range(len(l)):\n    ...:     d[l[i]]=l[(i+3)%26]\n    ...:\n\nIn [18]: print(d)\n{'a': 'd', 'b': 'e', 'c': 'f', 'd': 'g', 'e': 'h', 'f': 'i', 'g': 'j', 'h': 'k', 'i': 'l', 'j': 'm', 'k': 'n', 'l': 'o', 'm': 'p', 'n': 'q', 'o': 'r', 'p': 's', 'q': 't', 'r': 'u', 's': 'v', 't': 'w', 'u': 'x', 'v': 'y', 'w': 'z', 'x': 'a', 'y': 'b', 'z': 'c'}\n\nIn [19]: d['a']\nOut[19]: 'd'\n\nIn [20]: d['z']\nOut[20]: 'c'\n\nIn [21]: d['y']\nOut[21]: 'b'\n\nIn [22]: d['w']\nOut[22]: 'z'\n</code></pre>\n<p>The reading loop on the first word of the book, <code>chapter</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pass</th>\n<th><code>c</code> read</th>\n<th><code>d[c]</code> written</th>\n<th>Encrypted file so far</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>c</td>\n<td>f</td>\n<td><code>f</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>h</td>\n<td>k</td>\n<td><code>fk</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>a</td>\n<td>d</td>\n<td><code>fkd</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>p</td>\n<td>s</td>\n<td><code>fkds</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>t</td>\n<td>w</td>\n<td><code>fkdsw</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>e</td>\n<td>h</td>\n<td><code>fkdswh</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>r</td>\n<td>u</td>\n<td><code>fkdswhu</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>After the last letter of the book, <code>f.read(1)</code> gives <code>''</code>, and the loop stops. The program runs “without any errors” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>, and <code>encrypted_sherlock.<wbr>txt</code> begins with f, k, d: “C is becoming f … h is becoming k. A is becoming d. This was actually chapter” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>. Both files are 246 KB, “because it is letter by letter” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>: one character in, one character out.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A character that is not in the dictionary</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>d[c]</code> works only because <code>sherlock.txt</code> holds nothing but the 26 lowercase letters. A capital letter, a space or a newline in the file would stop the program with a <code>KeyError</code>, such as <code>KeyError: 'A'</code> (my own test), because the dictionary has no such key.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The dictionary is a <strong>function</strong> in the sense of <a href=\"#/maths1/w01/functions\">Mathematics 1, Week 1</a>: every letter has exactly one code letter. It is also <strong>one-to-one</strong> (two different letters never get the same code letter) and <strong>onto</strong> (every letter is the code of some letter). That is why the message can be decoded.</p>\n<a class=\"labcard\" href=\"#/labs/sets/functions?mode=finite&amp;by=arrows&amp;X=a,b,c,x,y,z&amp;Y=d,e,f,a,b,c&amp;f=a-&gt;d,b-&gt;e,c-&gt;f,x-&gt;a,y-&gt;b,z-&gt;c\"><span class=\"labcard-k\">Try it in the Sets and functions lab</span><span class=\"labcard-t\">Six arrows of the Caesar dictionary: one-to-one and onto, so it can be undone</span><span class=\"labcard-go\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></a>\n<p>To get the book back, you would “create another dictionary, which will do the subtraction of three letters the other side” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>. Writing that program, which takes <code>encrypted_sherlock.<wbr>txt</code> and gives back <code>sherlock.txt</code>, is the lecture’s homework <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>. The lecturer also mentions cryptanalysis, breaking such a cipher, for later <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkqFvnC2naE&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lecture-8-6-file-handling-genetic-sequences\">Lecture 8.6: File handling, Genetic Sequences</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span><span class=\"box-t\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>f.read(n)</code> reads the next <code>n</code> characters and <code>f.seek(k)</code> jumps to position <code>k</code>. A genome file of 5.5 MB of A, C, G and T is small enough to search with <code>in</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"jumping-inside-a-file-read-n-and-seek\">Jumping inside a file: read(n) and seek</h3>\n<p>The file <code>test.txt</code> holds the ten digits <code>0123456789</code> (the lecturer added the 0 at the start “so that it’s easy for you people”) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>. Then <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [6]: f=open('test.txt','r')\n\nIn [7]: s=f.read(2)\n\nIn [8]: print(s)\n01\n\nIn [9]: s=f.read(2)\n\nIn [10]: print(s)\n23\n\nIn [11]: s=f.read(2)\n\nIn [12]: print(s)\n45\n\nIn [13]: f.seek(4)\nOut[13]: 4\n\nIn [14]: s=f.read(2)\n\nIn [15]: print(s)\n45\n\nIn [16]: f.seek(2)\nOut[16]: 2\n\nIn [17]: s=f.read(1)\n\nIn [18]: print(s)\n2\n\nIn [19]: f.seek(7)\nOut[19]: 7\n\nIn [20]: f.read(1)\nOut[20]: '7'\n</code></pre>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Position, read(n) and seek</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>An open file keeps a <strong>position</strong>: how many characters from the start have been passed (here each character is one byte). Just after <code>open</code>, the position is 0.</li>\n<li><code>f.read(n)</code> reads the next <code>n</code> characters, starting at the position, and moves the position <code>n</code> places on.</li>\n<li><code>f.seek(k)</code> moves the position to <code>k</code>, counted from the start of the file, and gives back <code>k</code>. The next read starts there.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>In <code>test.txt</code>, the character at position <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span></span></span></span></span> is the digit <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span></span></span></span>,</span> which makes the positions easy to follow. <em>Before</em> and <em>After</em> are the positions before and after each command:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Before</th>\n<th>Gives</th>\n<th>After</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>f.read(2)</code></td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td><code>'01'</code></td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>f.read(2)</code></td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code>'23'</code></td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>f.read(2)</code></td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code>'45'</code></td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>f.seek(4)</code></td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code>4</code></td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>f.read(2)</code></td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code>'45'</code></td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>f.seek(2)</code></td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code>2</code></td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>f.read(1)</code></td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code>'2'</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>f.seek(7)</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code>7</code></td>\n<td>7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>f.read(1)</code></td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><code>'7'</code></td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“Basically, I am rewinding back the file” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">seek can be slow on a big file</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“f seek basically helps you go forward or go backward”, but “it does not go magically”: on the disk it may move “sort of linearly” to the new place, so “f seek can be costly”, depending on how the hard disk stores the data. “Use f seek judiciously” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"searching-a-genome-with-in\">Searching a genome with in</h3>\n<p>“Your entire genetic sequence of a human being comprises of A C T and G these 4 symbols only” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>. The lecturer’s simple picture (he says “I am not a biologist” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>): if a certain short piece appears in a person’s sequence, the person may be <strong>predisposed</strong> to a disease, that is, likely to get it <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>. The file <code>human.txt</code> is 5.5 MB of A, C, G and T <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [23]: f=open('human.txt','r')\n\nIn [24]: seq=f.read()\n\nIn [26]: seq[0]\nOut[26]: 'A'\n\nIn [27]: seq[1]\nOut[27]: 'T'\n\nIn [28]: seq[2]\nOut[28]: 'G'\n\nIn [29]: diab='GTATGAC'\n\nIn [30]: diab in seq\nOut[30]: True\n\nIn [31]: bp='TAGAAACCTGGATA'\n\nIn [32]: bp in seq\nOut[32]: False\n\nIn [33]: 'GTA' in seq\nOut[33]: True\n\nIn [34]: 'GTAC' in seq\nOut[34]: True\n\nIn [35]: 'AAAA' in seq\nOut[35]: True\n</code></pre>\n<ol>\n<li><code>seq=f.read()</code> puts the whole file into one string; <code>In [25]</code>, just <code>seq</code>, shows it, screen after screen <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>.</li>\n<li><code>seq[0]</code>, <code>seq[1]</code>, <code>seq[2]</code> are its first three letters.</li>\n<li><code>diab</code> is a made-up “diabetic gene”: “I just cooked that up, it will not be this small” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>. <code>diab in seq</code> is <code>True</code>: this piece appears in the sequence.</li>\n<li><code>bp</code> (a made-up blood-pressure sequence) does not appear: <code>False</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>.</li>\n<li>Short strings appear easily: “if you have a smaller string it will be there because it is a random sequence” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The lecturer keeps adding A’s <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>. Up to 11 A’s in a row the answer is <code>True</code>; with 12 it is <code>False</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [47]: 'AAAAAAAAAAAA' in seq\nOut[47]: False\n\nIn [48]: 'AAAAAAAAAAA' in seq\nOut[48]: True\n</code></pre>\n<p>So the longest run of A’s in this file is exactly 11 letters long.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The in operator on strings</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>You met <code>in</code> in <a href=\"#/python/w02/the-in-operator-and-chained-comparisons\">Week 2</a>. <code>t in s</code> is <code>True</code> when the string <code>t</code> appears inside the string <code>s</code> as a block of consecutive characters, a <strong>substring</strong>, and <code>False</code> otherwise. For example, <code>'GTA' in 'AGTAC'</code> is <code>True</code>, but <code>'GAC' in 'AGTAC'</code> is <code>False</code>: the letters are all there, but not next to each other (my own example).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">read() on a huge file</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>seq=f.read()</code> is fine for 5.5 MB. “If this file is a big one, it takes some a few days for it to enter into the memory” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>. For a file of several gigabytes, go through it in pieces, “letter by letter … by using f dot read 1”, or <code>f.read(n)</code>, where <code>n</code> “stands for the number of bytes that you are going to see” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>, or line by line as in 7.5.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Knuth–Morris–Pratt</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>For finding a substring in a very big string, the lecturer recommends the Knuth–Morris–Pratt (KMP) algorithm, “a very ingenious algorithm” and his favourite; it “is not part of your syllabus” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=df8FxNd27Dg&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"extra-lecture-not-on-seek-this-term\">Extra lecture (not on Seek this term)</h2>\n<h3 id=\"the-week-exceptions-and-functional-programming\">The week: exceptions and functional programming</h3>\n<p>A short video with one slide: “Exception handling, Functional programming”.</p>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>This week adds tools that you do not strictly need, but that make a programmer’s life easier. The lecturer lists them <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4HgUCharOBw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>: exception handling, and the “functional programming” features of Python: iterators, generators, inline statements, list comprehensions, lambda functions, enumerate, zip, map and filter. “None of these things are required to be a successful Python programmer” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4HgUCharOBw&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>, but using them is “one of those differences between a good programmer and an expert programmer” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4HgUCharOBw&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>. The functional tools are optional. Exception handling is not: it is “considered as industry standard for writing any code” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4HgUCharOBw&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Exception</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>exception</strong> is an error that happens while a program is running, such as reading from a closed file or taking the square root of a negative number. Python stops the program and prints a <strong>traceback</strong>: a report of where the error happened, which ends with the name of the error and a message, such as <code>ValueError: I/<wbr>O operation on closed file.</code> Read the last line first. <strong>Exception handling</strong> means writing code that catches such an error and decides what to do, instead of letting the program stop.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Where the other topics of this introduction are</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Exception handling with <code>try</code>, <code>except</code> and <code>finally</code> is lecture 9.11, on the <a href=\"#/python/w09\">Week 9</a> page. This week you meet the errors themselves: see <a href=\"#/python/w08/error-messages-of-this-week\">Error messages of this week</a>.</li>\n<li>Iterators, generators, lambda, enumerate, zip, map and filter (“functional programming”, parts 1 and 3) are Lectures 5.9 and 5.10, on the <a href=\"#/python/w05/lecture-5-9-iterators-and-generators\">Week 5</a> page, as on Seek.</li>\n<li>Inline statements and list comprehensions (“functional programming, part 2”) are lecture 4.7, on the <a href=\"#/python/w06\">Week 6</a> page.</li>\n<li>Lecture 8.1 promises pandas, a library for big data files; it comes in <a href=\"#/python/w11\">Week 11</a>.</li>\n<li>The videos are in playlist order, not teaching order. Lecture 8.1 is the <em>introduction</em> to file handling, and Lecture 5.9 (<a href=\"#/python/w05/lecture-5-9-iterators-and-generators\">Week 5</a>) talks about <code>readline</code> and <code>seek</code> as if you knew them already. They are explained below, in <a href=\"#/python/w08/lecture-8-4-very-big-files-a-tip\">very big files</a> and <a href=\"#/python/w08/jumping-inside-a-file-read-n-and-seek\">read(n) and seek</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"error-messages-of-this-week\">Error messages of this week</h2>\n<p>The errors shown in the lectures, in the order they appear:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>ValueError: I/<wbr>O operation on closed file.</code><br>\nIn 8.2, <code>x.read()</code> after <code>x.close()</code>. Read before you close.</li>\n<li><code>IndexError: list index out of range</code><br>\nIn 8.5, <code>l[26]</code>. The 26 letters have the indexes 0 to 25: use <code>% 26</code> to wrap around.</li>\n<li><code>NameError: name 's' is not defined</code><br>\nIn 8.3, <code>while(s!='')</code> before <code>s</code> had a value. Set <code>s='0'</code> before the loop.</li>\n<li><code>ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''</code><br>\nIn 8.3, <code>int(s)</code> at the end of the file. <code>''</code> is not a number: test <code>s!=''</code> first.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other errors around the same tools (my own tests, Python 3.14):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'nosuchfile.<wbr>txt'</code><br>\nOpening a missing file with <code>'r'</code>.</li>\n<li><code>io.<wbr>UnsupportedOperation: not writable</code><br>\nCalling <code>write</code> on a file opened with <code>'r'</code>.</li>\n<li><code>TypeError: write() argument must be str, not int</code><br>\nFrom <code>f.write(10)</code>: <code>write</code> takes strings only, so write <code>str(10)</code>.</li>\n<li><code>KeyError: 'A'</code><br>\nThe dictionary has no such key, such as a capital letter in the Caesar program.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>RAM and disk</strong> (8.1): RAM is fast, small (16 GB) and forgets at power-off; the hard disk is slow, big (1 TB) and permanent. Data too big to type is read from a file.</li>\n<li><strong>Open</strong> (8.2): <code>open(<wbr>name, 'r')</code> reads an existing file; <code>'w'</code> creates or empties it; <code>'a'</code> adds at the end. Always <code>close()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Write</strong>: <code>f.<wbr>write(<wbr>text)</code> gives back the number of characters and adds nothing else; <code>'\\n'</code> is one character, the new line.</li>\n<li><strong>Read</strong>: <code>f.read()</code> the whole file as one <code>str</code>; <code>f.readline()</code> the next line, ending in <code>'\\n'</code>; <code>f.read(n)</code> the next <code>n</code> characters; <code>f.seek(k)</code> jumps to position <code>k</code>. At the end of the file: <code>''</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Big files</strong> (8.4, 8.6): read line by line or a few characters at a time; then the size does not matter, only the time.</li>\n<li><strong>Caesar</strong> (8.5): <code>d[<wbr>l[i]]=<wbr>l[<wbr>(i+3)%26]</code>, code index <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.854em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.05724em;\">j</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">mod</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span></span></span></span>;</span> loop <code>c=f.read(1)</code> … <code>while (c!=<wbr>''):</code> write <code>d[c]</code>, read the next.</li>\n<li><strong>Search</strong> (8.3): start <code>s='0'</code>, loop <code>while(s!='')</code>, convert only if <code>s!=''</code>, a <code>flag</code> for “found”, <code>break</code> on a match.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">In 8.2, suppose the five <code>write</code> calls had no spaces: <code>'Sudarshan'</code>, <code>'yourname'</code>, <code>'IIT'</code>, <code>'python'</code>, <code>'India'</code>. What would they give back, and what would <code>print(s)</code> show after reading the file?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Each call gives back its number of characters: 9, 8, 3, 6 and 5.</li>\n<li>In total: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">9</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">17</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">17</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">31</span></span></span></span></span> characters.</li>\n<li><code>write</code> adds no spaces, so the file holds the words glued together.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 9, 8, 3, 6, 5; the file has 31 characters, and <code>print(s)</code> shows <code>SudarshanyournameIITpythonIndia</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">With the dictionary <code>d</code> of 8.5, what does <code>caesar.py</code> write for a file that contains only <code>zebra</code>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Each letter’s index <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6595em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span></span></span></span></span> goes to <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">mod</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span></span></span></span>:</span></p>\n<ol>\n<li>z: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6595em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">28</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">28</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">mod</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>,</span> which is c.</li>\n<li>e: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6595em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">mod</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span></span></span></span>,</span> which is h.</li>\n<li>b: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6595em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span></span></span></span>,</span> which is e.</li>\n<li>r: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6595em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">17</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">17</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span></span></span></span>,</span> which is u.</li>\n<li>a: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6595em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span></span></span></span>,</span> which is d.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>cheud</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The file <code>test.txt</code> of 8.6 holds <code>0123456789</code>. Just after <code>f=<wbr>open(<wbr>'test.<wbr>txt',<wbr>'r')</code>, what do <code>f.seek(3)</code>, then <code>f.read(4)</code>, then <code>f.read(2)</code> give?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><code>f.seek(3)</code> moves the position to 3 and gives back <code>3</code>.</li>\n<li><code>f.read(4)</code> reads the characters at positions 3, 4, 5 and 6: <code>'3456'</code>. The position becomes <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li><code>f.read(2)</code> reads positions 7 and 8: <code>'78'</code>. The position becomes <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">9</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>3</code>, <code>'3456'</code>, <code>'78'</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">In 8.3, the version of <code>findphone.py</code> without <code>if (s!=''):</code> printed “The number was found” for 9020214197 but stopped with a <code>ValueError</code> for 9020214198. Why?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>9020214197 is in the file. When its line is read, <code>n</code> matches, the program prints, sets <code>flag=1</code> and leaves the loop with <code>break</code>, before the end of the file is reached.</li>\n<li>9020214198 is not in the file, so no line matches and the loop reads to the end.</li>\n<li>There, <code>s=<wbr>f.<wbr>readline()</code> gives <code>''</code>, and the next line, <code>n=int(s)</code>, runs <strong>before</strong> the <code>while</code> test can see the <code>''</code>. <code>int('')</code> is not a number: <code>ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> only a search that reaches the end of the file reads <code>''</code>, and the old version converted it with <code>int</code>. The fix converts only when <code>s!=''</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
